The graph of $y = f(x)$ is shown.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-5.5, xmax=7.5, ymin=-2.5, ymax=7.5, clip=false]
\addplot[thick, <-, samples=2, domain=-4.8:-2] {-1};
\addplot[only marks, mark=*, mark options={fill=white}] coordinates {(-2,-1)};
\addplot[thick, samples=2, domain=-2:3] {x+3};
\addplot[only marks, mark=*] coordinates {(-2,1)};
\addplot[thick, ->, samples=2, domain=3:6.8] {9-x};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
At which value of $x$ is $f(x)$ continuous but not differentiable?

The graph of $y = \sqrt{4 - x^{2}}$ is shown.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-3.2, xmax=3.2, ymin=-1, ymax=3, samples=101, clip=false]
\addplot[smooth, thick, domain=-1.999:1.999] {sqrt(4-x*x)};
\addplot[only marks, mark=*] coordinates {(-2,0) (2,0)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Which statement about this function at $x = 2$ is correct?
